import crate::ParseError
import crate::parse::parser::Parser
import crate::parse::parser::test_decl::test_decl
import redox_diag::Code
import redox_lexer::{Keyword, TokenKind}

-- | Refuse a `test` declaration in a body position — `RDX0112`, plan 55
-- | leaf 6.
-- |
-- | The one carve-out from plan 55 §1.4, and it is rustc's call rather than a
-- | taste: a nested `#[test]` is **not collected**. §1.5 measured it — the
-- | `unnameable_test_items` lint warns `cannot test inner items`, the harness
-- | runs one test where two were written, and nothing fails. A construct that
-- | compiles and then silently does nothing is worse than one that is refused,
-- | because the author only finds out when the thing the test guarded breaks
-- | in production.
-- |
-- | # Asked at the statement, not in `prefix`
-- |
-- | `Parser::stmt` is where a body position begins, so one comparison at the
-- | top of it covers every body in the language — a clause's, a guard row's, a
-- | `where` list's, and every block a statement owns. `prefix` would catch the
-- | same keyword a token later and in a *worse* place: it is also the nested
-- | expression position, where `test` has no production at all and the
-- | generic *"expected an expression"* is the honest answer.
-- |
-- | # Why the whole declaration is read, and its own errors discarded
-- |
-- | The keyword alone would do for the diagnostic, and leaving the rest to
-- | recovery is what produced the status quo: a local `test` raised `RDX0102`
-- | on its keyword and then a **second** `RDX0102` on its indented body. Doc
-- | 05 §4's rule is that the first error names the actual defect, and a second
-- | error about the body of a construct that may not be there at all names
-- | nothing. So [`test_decl`] is run for its cursor movement, which leaves
-- | recovery to resume at the next statement.
-- |
-- | Its result is dropped, including a failure. A malformed local `test` has
-- | two faults and the position is the one worth reporting: an author told to
-- | quote the description would fix it and arrive right back here. The old
-- | behaviour is the warning — `test adds` in a body reported *"expected the
-- | test's description"*, which is true, actionable, and about the wrong
-- | problem.
export(crate) local_test : &mut Parser -> Result<(), ParseError>
local_test p =
  if !.check (&TokenKind::Kw Keyword::Test) p
    return Ok ()
  -- `Vec::new ()` for the leading trivia, because the node is dropped — the
  -- one way this differs from `Parser::item`'s call, and sound because
  -- nothing downstream ever sees it.
  keyword = .peek_span p
  -- `_dropped`, where the Rust wrote `let _ = …`: `_` is not a Redox pattern,
  -- and the binding is still what satisfies `Result`'s `#[must_use]`. A
  -- `drop (…)` would do it too and draws `clippy::drop_non_drop`.
  _dropped = test_decl p (Vec::new ()) keyword.0
  Err (ParseError::with_help Code::LocalTest "a `test` declaration belongs at module scope, and this one is inside a body" "move it out to the left margin: rustc does not collect a nested `#[test]` — it warns `cannot test inner items` and the test silently never runs (plan 55 §1.5)" keyword)
